# TRASH SAYS HOW LONG IT KEEPS THINGS, AND ONLY TRASH CAN.
#
# `purge_window_days` is the WINDOW — "purged 30 days after deletion" — and it
# is 0 on every other shelf. The three other `photos-shelf` fixtures beside this
# one leave it unset for exactly that reason: the field is not "a retention
# sentence a shelf may have", it is the one sentence the trash predicate earns.
# A shelf that could carry it under any predicate is a shelf that eventually
# tells a member their album expires — which is how v0's four separate shelf
# screens drifted in the first place, one of them growing an affordance the
# others could not show.
#
# IT WAS `purge_in_days`, A COUNTDOWN, AND NOTHING COULD PRODUCE IT. `PhotoCell`
# carries no `purge_at`, so a row's own deadline never reaches the state, and
# `commonMain` has no clock BY DESIGN (`sync/Instants.kt`) — so a reducer could
# not subtract one from today even if it did. The window needs neither, and it
# is what v0's trash meta line actually said.
#
# 30 is `PURGE_AFTER_DAYS` in `crates/vault/src/commands/media.rs`, NOT
# `retentionWindows` in `contracts/schema/v0-registries.json` — that object
# holds only `audit` and `ledger`.
seat {
  availability: AVAILABILITY_READY
  durability: DURABILITY_AUTHORITATIVE
  connectivity: CONNECTIVITY_ONLINE_UNMETERED
}
shelf {
  state_view {
    mode { kind: KIND_TRASH }
  }
}
data {
  cells {
    asset_id: "ast-0101"
    thumbnail_path: "thumbs/ast-0101.jpg"
